# ctp-bridge configuration — copy to .ctp-bridge.conf on the box and edit.
#
# This file is the BOUNDARY. The wrapper (scripts/ctp-bridge.sh) and the
# PreToolUse hook (.claude/hooks/pretooluse-ctp.sh) both read it, and neither
# trusts caller-supplied environment for these values — an agent that can set an
# env var in the same call it makes could otherwise rewrite its own limits.
#
# .ctp-bridge.conf is gitignored: it lives on the box and is never committed. It
# holds NO secrets — only a box name, a verb list, and paths to deny. Real
# secret VALUES never appear here.
#
# Format: KEY=value, one per line. Lines starting with # are ignored. No shell
# expansion is performed on values.

# Explicit hostname override: space-separated hostnames this harness may deploy
# to WITHOUT querying the inventory. Use for custom-named hosts that do not
# appear in any team's Providentia inventory. A glob metacharacter is refused.
# Leave empty to rely entirely on the live inventory query (the normal case).
CTP_ALLOWED_TARGET=

# Enclave allowlist: space-separated enclave suffixes (the last hyphen-delimited
# segment of a hostname, e.g. "hn" matches mail-hn, dc-hn). A target whose
# suffix is not in this list is refused before the team query runs (fail fast).
# Targets in CTP_ALLOWED_TARGET bypass the enclave check.
# Leave empty to skip the enclave gate (backward compatible).
CTP_ALLOWED_ENCLAVES=

# Authorised team numbers: comma-separated list of team numbers this harness
# may deploy to (e.g. "10" or "10,2"). A mutating target is queried via
# ansible-inventory on the catapult container — the returned actor_nr_str must
# match any entry in this list. Targets in CTP_ALLOWED_TARGET bypass the query.
# Fail closed: if this is unset, every mutating verb is refused.
CTP_ALLOWED_TEAM_NR=

# Deprecated — kept for backward compatibility. If CTP_ALLOWED_TEAM_NR is unset
# and this is set, its value is treated as a team number (same semantics as
# CTP_ALLOWED_TEAM_NR). Prefer CTP_ALLOWED_TEAM_NR for new installs.
# CTP_ALLOWED_TEAM=

# The MUTATING verbs the wrapper will run. Allow-list: a verb not named here is
# refused. Read verbs (host list <box>, project update-inventory) are reachable
# separately and always confirmed; host vars, redeploy and remove are not
# reachable, and secrets/make are refused outright regardless of this line.
CTP_ALLOWED_VERBS=deploy deploy-role

# Container override. Leave blank to derive catapult-<current-user> at runtime.
CTP_CONTAINER=

# Optional: the project directory INSIDE the container that ctp runs from (where
# playbook.yml lives), e.g. /srv/inventories/<project>. Leave blank to translate
# the directory you invoke the wrapper from through the container's bind mount at
# runtime (run the wrapper from your range checkout). Set this only to pin a fixed
# project regardless of CWD.
CTP_PROJECT_DIR=

# Inventory path inside the catapult container. Used by the live team query
# (ansible-inventory --host). Default: /srv/inventories/dcm/inventory/
CTP_INVENTORY_PATH=

# Secret paths the hook refuses to let any tool read (Read tool or a Bash command
# naming the path), so their contents never reach the transcript. Space-separated
# glob patterns. Tilde expands to the target user's home. Extend per box; never
# remove the vault password or private-key entries.
CTP_SECRET_PATHS=/var/tmp/vlt_pf ~/.ssh/id_* ~/.zsh_history ~/.bash_history **/.env

# Org PII / intellectual-property paths the hook refuses to let any tool read OR
# write (Read/Write/Edit, or a Bash command naming the path), so Org data never
# reaches Claude's transcript. Same glob syntax as CTP_SECRET_PATHS (tilde
# expands to the target home; a **/ prefix matches anywhere by basename).
#
# Separate from CTP_SECRET_PATHS on purpose: secrets are credentials (never
# touch); these are a data-governance boundary you tune independently. Hooks
# cannot redact tool OUTPUT, so the only reliable protection for a file's
# contents is to deny the read here — list the directories where Org data lives.
#
# EMPTY IS THE DEFAULT (opt-in): nothing is denied until you add paths. Example:
#   CTP_PII_PATHS=~/org-data/** ~/cases/**/*.csv /srv/customer/**
#
# If you run the local-model orchestrator, also list your TUNED classifier and
# sanitiser prompts here — they accumulate the sensitive patterns they are built
# to catch, so Claude's tools must not read them (the orchestrator reads them
# directly and still works):
# NOTE: everything in ~/.config/orchestrator/ (classifier prompt, sanitiser
# prompt, executor prompt, term list, private eval fixtures, the handle-to-path
# map) is guarded ALWAYS,
# without being listed here, and that cannot be switched off from this file.
# List only YOUR OWN data paths below.
#   CTP_PII_PATHS=~/org-data/** /srv/customer/**
CTP_PII_PATHS=

# Remote execution: SSH host alias for the spoke that runs the catapult
# container. When set, docker commands route through `ssh <host>` instead of
# running locally. Use this in a devcontainer where docker is not available
# but the spoke is reachable via SSH (e.g. ProxyJump through hub).
# The alias must be resolvable from ~/.ssh/config (see setup-container-ssh.sh).
# When CTP_REMOTE_HOST is set, CTP_PROJECT_DIR is REQUIRED — CWD translation
# through a remote bind mount is not supported.
CTP_REMOTE_HOST=
